'''Aaron Darnell Ford''' (born May 24, 1972) is an American lawyer and politician currently serving as the 34th [[Nevada Attorney General|attorney general of Nevada]] since 2019. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], he previously served as a member of the [[Nevada Senate]] representing the [[Nevada's 11th Senate district|11th district]] from 2013 to 2018.<ref name="Mueller2024GovernorBid">{{Cite news |last=Mueller |first=Tabitha |date=December 12, 2024 |title=Nevada Attorney General Aaron Ford says he intends to run for governor |url=https://thenevadaindependent.com/article/nevada-attorney-general-aaron-ford-says-he-intends-to-run-for-governor |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241212174015/https://thenevadaindependent.com/article/nevada-attorney-general-aaron-ford-says-he-intends-to-run-for-governor |archive-date=December 12, 2024 |work=[[The Nevada Independent]]}}</ref> He served as Nevada Senate [[Majority leader|majority]] and [[minority leader]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Lacanlale |first=Rio |date=October 19, 2022 |title=Nevada attorney general race pits reform-minded incumbent against 'tough-on-crime' GOP lawyer |url=https://www.rgj.com/story/news/2022/10/19/election-2022-nevada-attorney-general-race-aaron-ford-sigal-chattah/8129182001/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250507034541/https://www.rgj.com/story/news/2022/10/19/election-2022-nevada-attorney-general-race-aaron-ford-sigal-chattah/8129182001/ |archive-date=May 7, 2025 |work=[[Reno Gazette-Journal]]}}</ref>

Ford defeated [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] nominee [[Wesley Duncan]] in the [[2018 Nevada Attorney General election|2018 Nevada attorney general election]], winning by just 0.4% of the vote in a close race.<ref name="FordBeatsDuncan">{{Cite news |last=Lochhead |first=Colton |date=November 6, 2018 |title=Aaron Ford edges out Wes Duncan to win attorney general race |url=https://www.reviewjournal.com/news/politics-and-government/nevada/aaron-ford-edges-out-wes-duncan-to-win-attorney-general-race-1521215/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181109155410/https://www.reviewjournal.com/news/politics-and-government/nevada/aaron-ford-edges-out-wes-duncan-to-win-attorney-general-race-1521215/ |archive-date=November 9, 2018 |work=[[Las Vegas Review-Journal]]}}</ref> He was reelected in [[2022 Nevada Attorney General election|2022]], winning reelection by the largest victory margin for a statewide Democratic [[incumbent]] in Nevada history against Republican nominee [[Sigal Chattah]].<ref name="Lacanlale2022">{{Cite news |last=Lacanlale |first=Rio |date=November 8, 2022 |title=Attorney General Aaron Ford holds lead over Republican Sigal Chattah |url=https://www.rgj.com/story/news/2022/11/08/nevada-midterm-election-results-who-emerges-tight-attorney-generals-race-sigal-chattah-aaron-ford/10624917002/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250507021640/https://www.rgj.com/story/news/2022/11/08/nevada-midterm-election-results-who-emerges-tight-attorney-generals-race-sigal-chattah-aaron-ford/10624917002/ |archive-date=May 7, 2025 |work=[[Reno Gazette-Journal]]}}</ref>

Ford is the Democratic nominee for [[governor of Nevada]] in the [[2026 Nevada gubernatorial election|2026 election]]. He faces Republican incumbent [[Joe Lombardo]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hill |first=Jessica |date=December 12, 2024 |title=AG Aaron Ford confirms his 2026 bid for governor |url=https://www.reviewjournal.com/news/politics-and-government/nevada/ag-ford-confirms-his-2026-bid-for-governor-3227314/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241212235755/https://www.reviewjournal.com/news/politics-and-government/nevada/ag-ford-confirms-his-2026-bid-for-governor-3227314/ |archive-date=December 12, 2024 |work=[[Las Vegas Review-Journal]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Hartman |first=Jim |date=May 3, 2025 |title=Lombardo vs. Sisolak: A gubernatorial rematch? |url=https://www.nevadaappeal.com/news/2025/may/03/jim-hartman-lombardo-vs-sisolak-a-gubernatorial-rematch/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250507050437/https://www.nevadaappeal.com/news/2025/may/03/jim-hartman-lombardo-vs-sisolak-a-gubernatorial-rematch/ |archive-date=May 7, 2025 |work=[[Nevada Appeal]]}}</ref>

== Early life and education ==

Ford was born in [[Dallas, Texas]] and raised in [[Houston|Houston, Texas]].<ref name="rise" /><ref name="Website-1">{{Cite web |last=Meyerson |first=Harold |date=2026-10-13 |title=Can Nevada Democrats Roll a Trifecta? |url=https://prospect.org/2026/10/13/nevada-democrats-governor-aaron-ford-working-class/ |access-date=2026-10-20 |website=The American Prospect |language=en-US}}</ref> He earned a scholarship and received his undergraduate degree from [[Texas A&M University]] in 1994.<ref name="rise">{{Cite news |last=Rindels |first=Michelle |date=January 18, 2017 |title=Aaron Ford's rise to the Senate pinnacle, from food stamps to floor leader |url=https://thenevadaindependent.com/article/food-stamps-floor-leader-aaron-fords-rise-senate-pinnacle |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170918172425/https://thenevadaindependent.com/article/food-stamps-floor-leader-aaron-fords-rise-senate-pinnacle/ |archive-date=September 18, 2017 |work=[[The Nevada Independent]]}}</ref> He was the first in his family to graduate from college. He earned a master's degree in international education from [[George Washington University]]. He also has an [[Master of Arts|M.A.]], [[Juris Doctor|J.D.]], and [[Doctor of Philosophy|Ph.D.]] from [[Ohio State University]].<ref name="Lochhead2018">{{Cite news |last=Lochhead |first=Colton |date=October 24, 2018 |title=Son's joke spurred Aaron Ford's run for office |url=https://www.reviewjournal.com/news/politics-and-government/nevada/sons-joke-spurred-aaron-fords-run-for-office/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181106190119/https://www.reviewjournal.com/news/politics-and-government/nevada/sons-joke-spurred-aaron-fords-run-for-office/ |archive-date=November 6, 2018 |access-date=May 7, 2025 |work=[[Las Vegas Review-Journal]]}}</ref>

=== Legal issues ===

During his college years between 1991 and 1994, Ford was arrested four times on [[misdemeanor]] charges, though these arrests did not result in convictions.<ref name="Robison2022">{{Cite news |last=Robison |first=Mark |date=October 21, 2022 |title=RGJ fact checker: Sigal Chattah makes false claim about Aaron Ford conviction |url=https://www.rgj.com/story/news/politics/elections/2022/10/21/sigal-chattah-false-claim-attorney-general-aaron-ford-conviction-rgj-fact-checker-nevada-election/10556440002/ |access-date=November 1, 2022 |work=Reno Gazette Journal |language=en-US}}</ref> The specific citations included public intoxication, failure to appear for a traffic violation, and a theft-related offense; the latter was dismissed after he paid restitution.<ref name="Giwargis2018">{{Cite news |date=July 27, 2018 |last=Giwargis |first=Ramona |title=Nevada AG candidate Aaron Ford arrested 4 times in 1990s |url=https://www.reviewjournal.com/news/politics-and-government/nevada/nevada-ag-candidate-aaron-ford-arrested-4-times-in-1990s/ |access-date=October 11, 2018 |work=[[Las Vegas Review-Journal]] |language=en-US}}</ref> Ford has publicly addressed this history, characterizing the incidents as "youthful mistakes" that provided learning experiences. He stated that he did not wish to be judged solely "on the first 20 years" of his life.<ref name="Sebelius2018">{{Cite news |last=Sebelius |first=Steve |date=July 27, 2018 |title=AG candidate state senator Aaron Ford discusses arrest in 1990s |url=https://www.8newsnow.com/news/local-news/ag-candidate-state-sen-aaron-ford-discusses-arrests-in-1990s/1328936627/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250507051604/https://www.8newsnow.com/news/local-news/democratic-nevada-ag-nominee-addresses-old-irs-liens/1321882987/ |archive-date=May 7, 2025 |work=[[KLAS-TV]]}}</ref>

== Teaching and legal career ==

Ford has worked as a public school teacher.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Chouinard |first1=Kyle |date=21 March 2025 |title=Nevada AG blasts governor over support for dismantling Education Department |url=https://lasvegassun.com/news/2025/mar/21/nevada-ag-blasts-governor-over-support-for-dismant/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250323122434/https://lasvegassun.com/news/2025/mar/21/nevada-ag-blasts-governor-over-support-for-dismant/ |archive-date=March 23, 2025 |access-date=27 June 2025 |work=[[Las Vegas Sun]] |language=en}}</ref> He clerked for federal judges [[Denise Page Hood]] and [[Johnnie B. Rawlinson]] on the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit|Ninth Circuit Court of Appeals]].<ref>{{cite web |title=A Conversation on Voting Rights in Nevada and Around the Country with Attorney General Aaron D. Ford {{!}} William S. Boyd School of Law |url=https://law.unlv.edu/events/conversation-voting-rights-nevada-and-around-country-attorney-general-aaron-d-ford |publisher=UNLV Law School |access-date=27 June 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

After completing his [[Law clerk|clerkships]], he practiced law at firms in Texas and Nevada.  Ford moved back to Texas to work at [[Bracewell LLP]] as an associate. Later, he became an associate at [[Weil, Gotshal & Manges|Weil Gotshal & Manges]]. Upon moving back to Las Vegas, Ford joined [[Snell & Wilmer]] and was promoted to partner a few years later. He has also worked at Eglet Prince.<ref name="Ritter2019">{{cite news |last1=Ritter |first1=Ken |date=April 24, 2019 |title=Nevada hires attorney general's old firm for opioids lawsuit |url=https://lasvegassun.com/news/2019/apr/24/nevada-hires-attorney-generals-old-firm-for-opioid/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190425162027/https://lasvegassun.com/news/2019/apr/24/nevada-hires-attorney-generals-old-firm-for-opioid/ |archive-date=April 25, 2019 |access-date=June 27, 2025 |work=[[Las Vegas Sun]] |language=en |agency=[[Associated Press]]}}</ref>

== Political career ==

=== Nevada Senate ===

Ford ran for [[Nevada Senate]] in District 12 in 2010 but lost to the Republican incumbent, [[Joe Hardy (politician)|Joe Hardy]], by around 20 points.<ref name="AaronFordBallotpedia">{{Cite news |title=Aaron Ford (Nevada) |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Aaron_Ford |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250503232204/https://ballotpedia.org/Aaron_Ford_(Nevada) |archive-date=May 3, 2025 |access-date=July 18, 2018 |website=[[Ballotpedia]] |language=}}</ref>

After relocating in 2011, Ford ran for the Senate again in District 11 in the 2012 election, defeating Republican incumbent, John Drake, by 24.6%.<ref name="AaronFordBallotpedia" />

In 2013, Ford served as the Assistant Majority [[Whip (politics)|Whip]] and in 2015 became the [[Minority leader|Minority Leader]] of the Senate.<ref name="AaronFordBallotpedia" /> In 2017, Ford became the [[Majority leader|Majority Leader]] after the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]] regained control of the [[Nevada Assembly]].<ref name="rise" />

In 2017 Nevada legislative session, Ford led the efforts requiring law enforcement agencies to turn over [[rape kits]] for testing within 30 days and require labs to test them within 120 days. The bill mandated the creation of a Nevada-wide sexual assault kit tracking program, which would allow victims to follow the status of their rape kits as they go through the criminal justice system.<ref name="GiwargisFeb2018">{{Cite news |last=Giwargis |first=Ramona |date=February 27, 2018 |title=More rape kits in Southern Nevada being tested after new law |url=https://www.reviewjournal.com/news/more-rape-kits-in-southern-nevada-being-tested-after-new-law/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180228081033/https://www.reviewjournal.com/news/more-rape-kits-in-southern-nevada-being-tested-after-new-law/ |archive-date=February 28, 2018 |work=[[Las Vegas Review-Journal]]}}</ref>

During the same session, Ford helped pass a stricter [[Medication costs|drug pricing]] transparency law.<ref name="Pflanzer2017">{{Cite web |last=Pflanzer |first=Lydia Ramsey |title=Nevada just passed one of the strictest drug pricing transparency laws in the country |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/nevada-passes-insulin-drug-pricing-transparency-bill-2017-6 |access-date=2025-05-07 |website=Business Insider |language=en-US}}</ref> He also authored legislation requiring [[Financial adviser|financial advisors]] to be held accountable to a [[Fiduciary|fiduciary standard]], a higher threshold than the previous suitability standard.<ref name="Weinberg2019">{{Cite news |last=Weinberg |first=Neil |date=March 12, 2019 |title=The Battle Over Broker Rules Goes Local |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2019-03-12/the-battle-over-broker-rules-goes-local |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190413184307/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2019-03-12/the-battle-over-broker-rules-goes-local |archive-date=April 13, 2019 |work=[[Bloomberg News]]}}</ref> Ford stated that the fiduciary law was motivated by his experience representing an elderly client who had been "ripped off" by an insurance salesman.<ref name="Thrasher2017">{{Cite news |last=Thrasher |first=Michael |date=June 26, 2017 |title=Other States Considering Their Own ‘Fiduciary Rules' After Nevada’s Becomes Law |url=https://www.wealthmanagement.com/wealth-management-industry-trends/other-states-considering-their-own-fiduciary-rules-after-nevada-s-becomes-law |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250515013024/https://www.wealthmanagement.com/wealth-management-industry-trends/other-states-considering-their-own-fiduciary-rules-after-nevada-s-becomes-law |archive-date=2025-05-15 |access-date=2025-12-09 |work=Wealth Management |language=en}}</ref>
[[File:Nevada Attorney General Aaron Ford ICCPR Civil Society Consultation October 17-18, 2023.jpg|thumb|Ford in 2023 answers a question at an event before addressing the United Nations Human Rights Committee as part of delegation from the U.S. including federal, state, and local leadership and law enforcement.]]
During his time in the Senate, Ford served as the chair of the Committee on Natural Resources, vice chair of the Committee on Education, and was a member of the Senate Judiciary Committee.<ref name="AaronFordBallotpedia" />

=== Nevada attorney general ===
{{See also|2018 Nevada Attorney General election|2022 Nevada Attorney General election}}
In 2018, Ford ran for and was elected as Nevada's 34th attorney general, becoming the first African American to hold a statewide constitutional office in the state. He assumed office on January 7, 2019, and was re-elected in 2022, defeating Republican nominee Sigal Chattah with 52.3% of the vote (511,263 votes) compared to her 44.4% (434,084 votes).<ref name="Lacanlale2022" />

Ford secured more than $1.1 billion in [[Settlement (litigation)|settlements]] for Nevada to combat and treat the [[opioid epidemic]] as part of a series of lawsuits he filed against [[opioid]] [[Pharmaceutical industry|manufacturers]] and [[Pharmacy|distributors]] for their role in [[Drug overdose|overdose deaths]] and healthcare system costs paid by taxpayers. In January 2022, he announced the state would receive over $285 million from a collection of settlements with major distributors [[Cencora|AmerisourceBergen]], Cardinal Health, and [[McKesson Corporation|McKesson]], as well as manufacturer Johnson & Johnson.<ref name="Snyder2022">{{Cite news |last=Snyder |first=Riley |date=January 4, 2022 |title=Nevada to receive $285 million in latest round of opioid settlements |url=https://thenevadaindependent.com/article/nevada-to-receive-285-million-in-latest-round-of-opioid-settlements |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230705230949/https://thenevadaindependent.com/article/nevada-to-receive-285-million-in-latest-round-of-opioid-settlements |archive-date=July 5, 2023 |work=[[The Nevada Independent]]}}</ref> In 2023, Ford announced the state's settlement agreement of $285 million with [[Walgreens]] for its role in contributing to the opioid crisis by distributing the drug. The payments to the state are to take place over the course of 15 years beginning in 2023.<ref name="Neugeboren2023">{{Cite news |last=Neugeboren |first=Eric |date=July 5, 2023 |title=Nevada to receive $285 million from Walgreens; opioid settlements now top $1.1 billion |url=https://thenevadaindependent.com/article/nevada-to-receive-285-million-from-walgreens-opioid-settlements-now-top-1-1-billion |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230705222115/https://thenevadaindependent.com/article/nevada-to-receive-285-million-from-walgreens-opioid-settlements-now-top-1-1-billion |archive-date=July 5, 2023 |work=[[The Nevada Independent]]}}</ref>

During his tenure as Senate majority leader and later as attorney general, Ford accepted sponsored travel funded by outside organizations, primarily the [[Attorney General Alliance]]. Financial disclosures indicate that between 2017 and 2025, Ford accepted trips to destinations including Israel, Ghana, Qatar, Spain, South Africa, Poland, and South Korea, with a total reported value exceeding $140,000.<ref name="Golonka2024">{{Cite news |last=Golonka |first=Sean |date=February 18, 2024 |title=AG Ford, Speaker Yeager are top Nevada recipients of trips paid for by outside groups |url=https://thenevadaindependent.com/article/ag-ford-speaker-yeager-are-top-nevada-recipients-of-trips-paid-for-by-outside-groups |work=[[The Nevada Independent]] |access-date=December 14, 2025}}</ref><ref name=hill>{{cite news |last1=Hill |first1=Jessica |title=Nevada Attorney General Aaron Ford spent one-third of 2024 out of state |url=https://www.reviewjournal.com/investigations/nevada-attorney-general-aaron-ford-spent-one-third-of-2024-out-of-state-3483005/ |access-date=16 December 2025 |work=Las Vegas Review-Journal |date=8 October 2025}}</ref> The AGA is funded largely by corporate sponsorships, including from companies subject to state regulations and litigation, drawing criticism regarding potential conflicts of interest.<ref name="Golonka2024"/><ref>{{cite news |last1=Abou-Ghazala |first1=Yahya |title=State attorneys general take luxurious trips with corporate lawyers and lobbyists, raising questions of influence|url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/03/27/politics/attorney-general-luxury-foreign-trips-invs |access-date=16 December 2025 |work=CNN |date=27 March 2025 |language=en}}</ref> Ford spent one-third of 2024 out of state.<ref name="hill" />

In February 2026, an ethics complaint against Ford over sponsored trips and official social media posts advanced past the initial review stage. Opened in September 2025 by Ross Armstrong, executive director of the [[Nevada Commission on Ethics|state ethics commission]], a review panel unanimously concluded on February 11 that enough evidence existed to bring the complaint to the full commission. The complaint raised two specific concerns: the use of official Attorney General social media accounts to amplify private campaign accounts and the acceptance of international trips valued over $35,000 in 2023-2024 from the Attorney General Alliance.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Robison |first=Mark |title=Nevada ethics panel advances complaint over Aaron Ford 'luxury' trips |url=https://www.rgj.com/story/news/politics/2026/02/17/ethics-complaint-against-nevada-attorney-general-aaron-ford-advances/88724587007/ |access-date=2026-03-09 |website=Reno Gazette Journal |language=en-US}}</ref>

Ford pursued litigation against several online platforms, including [[Roblox]], [[Meta Platforms|Meta]], TikTok, [[Snapchat]], YouTube and [[Kik (app)|Kik]], alleging that they failed to protect underage users.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news |last=Hill |first=Jessica |date=April 15, 2026 |title=Roblox gaming platform reaches $12 million settlement with Nevada enhancing youth protections |url=https://apnews.com/article/roblox-nevada-settlement-28b3d7d7a483dc28462a7504b67c9bbc |access-date=July 31, 2026 |work=[[Associated Press]]}}</ref> In April 2026, he announced a settlement with Roblox. The settlement required Roblox to pay $12 million to the state of Nevada, implement protections for minors, and fund programs such as the [[Boys & Girls Clubs of America]].<ref name=":0" />

=== 2026 Nevada gubernatorial election ===
{{See also|2026 Nevada gubernatorial election}}
In December 2024, Ford confirmed his candidacy for [[List of governors of Nevada|governor of Nevada]] in the [[2026 Nevada gubernatorial election|2026 election]], challenging Republican incumbent [[Joe Lombardo]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hill |first=Jessica |date=December 12, 2024 |title=AG Aaron Ford confirms his 2026 bid for governor |url=https://www.reviewjournal.com/news/politics-and-government/nevada/ag-ford-confirms-his-2026-bid-for-governor-3227314/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241212235755/https://www.reviewjournal.com/news/politics-and-government/nevada/ag-ford-confirms-his-2026-bid-for-governor-3227314/ |archive-date=December 12, 2024 |work=[[Las Vegas Review-Journal]]}}</ref> Ford, who is [[Term limit|term-limited]] from seeking re-election as attorney general, officially launched his campaign in July 2025; his entry into the race followed months of speculation regarding a potential rematch between Lombardo and former governor [[Steve Sisolak]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hartman |first=Jim |date=May 3, 2025 |title=Lombardo vs. Sisolak: A gubernatorial rematch? |url=https://www.nevadaappeal.com/news/2025/may/03/jim-hartman-lombardo-vs-sisolak-a-gubernatorial-rematch/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250507050437/https://www.nevadaappeal.com/news/2025/may/03/jim-hartman-lombardo-vs-sisolak-a-gubernatorial-rematch/ |archive-date=May 7, 2025 |work=[[Nevada Appeal]]}}</ref>
== Personal life ==

Ford is married to Berna Rhodes, an attorney. They have three sons, and are also raising their nephew.<ref name="rise" /> The couple met in college.<ref name="Website-1" />

On July 21, 2018, the ''[[Las Vegas Review-Journal]]'' reported that Ford "has a history of failing to fully pay his taxes."<ref name="Liens">{{Cite news |date=July 22, 2018 |last=Lochhead |first=Colton |title=Nevada AG candidate Aaron Ford had past liens for unpaid taxes |url=https://www.reviewjournal.com/news/politics-and-government/nevada/nevada-ag-candidate-aaron-ford-had-past-liens-for-unpaid-taxes/ |access-date=October 11, 2018 |work=[[Las Vegas Review-Journal]] |language=en-US}}</ref> According to Clark County records, the [[Internal Revenue Service|IRS]] filed three tax liens against Ford totaling more than $185,000 in unpaid taxes, interest, and penalties for the years 2010 through 2013. The liens were cleared in 2016.<ref name="Liens" /> Ford's campaign manager, Peggy Yang, stated that Ford had "faced some financial difficulties" resulting from the [[Great Recession|recession]], which were further complicated by the tax implications of his promotion to partner at his law firm.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Sebelius |first=Steve |date=July 25, 2018 |title=Democratic Nevada AG nominee addresses old IRS liens |url=https://www.lasvegasnow.com/news/local-news/democratic-nevada-ag-nominee-addresses-old-irs-liens/1321882987 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250507051604/https://www.8newsnow.com/news/local-news/democratic-nevada-ag-nominee-addresses-old-irs-liens/1321882987/ |archive-date=May 7, 2025 |access-date=October 13, 2018 |website=[[KLAS-TV]] |language=en-US}}</ref> The ''Review-Journal'' noted that Ford purchased a new home for $468,138 in 2013, one of the years he failed to fully pay his income taxes. Ford responded that he had "short-sold a house during that time period."<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://knpr.org/knpr/2018-09/aaron-ford-his-run-nevada-attorney-general|title=Aaron Ford On His Run For Nevada Attorney General|work=State of Nevada, [[Nevada Public Radio]]|access-date=October 13, 2018|language=en}}</ref>

